M/Hasan. A.B.O 
","The PETITIONER, a company engaged in breaking and recycling ships, moved the court under Article 102 of the Constitution complaining of a communication of the customs authorities of 01.11.2022, by which its application of 13.01.2022 for return of customs duty and yard value added tax paid on an imported vessel brought for scrapping was turned down, and seeking a direction on the authorities to return the money. The PETITIONER pleaded that its agreement for purchase with a foreign seller was cancelled because the vessel could not be delivered, that it never took possession and played no part in the vessel leaving the port, and that duties were later taken again from a second buyer of the same vessel. The STATE side pleaded that the procedure for re-exportation was not followed and that an appeal lay.","Other: Customs Act, 1969",ALLOWED,"The court held that the provision on frustrated cargo contemplates an application by the consignor with the approval of the Board, and found that no such application was ever made, the port clearance having been issued by the customs house itself, so that provision had no application to the PETITIONER's claim; it found that neither the order in the admiralty suit nor the settlement between the parties barred a return of the money, and that realising duty twice over the same vessel was arbitrary. It held the writ maintainable because only questions of interpretation arose, and directed the authorities to return the duty.",15eeaa160f315c210a91663829cd4b4647c9fcaee5b3258cfef21e201ae9e690
