","The PETITIONERS, defendants in a partition suit, assailed the judgment and decree dated 27.04.2002 of the Joint District Judge (Artha Rin), Dinajpur in a class appeal, by which the appellate court declined to disturb the judgment and decree dated 20.07.2000 of the Senior Assistant Judge, Additional Court No.1, Dinajpur, passed in the partition suit. The OPPOSITE PARTIES, as plaintiffs, traced their right by inheritance through a common ancestor and his widow's second marriage, and sought allotment of their shares in the joint holding, pleading refusal of an amicable division. The PETITIONERS denied the plaintiffs' descent, claimed that the record of rights stood differently, and asserted that the plaintiffs had come to live on the land with their permission. No one appeared for the PETITIONERS at the hearing.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,REFUSED,"The court examined the judgments of both the courts below and found that the trial court had allotted shares on a consideration of the evidence and that the appellate court had concurrently found that the plaintiffs had been living on the suit property without any cogent evidence that they did so with the permission of the defendants. It observed that the suit land was held by the parties jointly, so a partition was required, and that both sides had been given their respective portions. Finding no error of law in the concurrent decisions, it declined to interfere with the preliminary decree.",3566b1ef21e539477613e4a8d2467c6a880d3ff4ac5f93abee20ae3c7c26187d
