","The PETITIONERS, defendants in a title suit, resisted a claim founded on a written agreement for sale of land executed in 1979, under which a substantial part of the price was paid and the land was handed over, the OPPOSITE PARTIES suing as plaintiffs for a deed of sale and pleading that the PETITIONERS afterwards declined to execute it and threatened them with dispossession. The PETITIONERS denied the agreement, pleaded that the paper had been fabricated out of signed stamp papers connected with a loan dispute, and relied on their own record of rights, on a later gift and on their possession of parts of the holding. The trial court decided the suit against the plaintiffs, while the appellate court decided the appeal in their favour. In revision the contest turned on the measurement of the holding and the effect of several earlier transfers between the parties.",Specific Relief Act 1877,PARTLY,"The court found that the trial court had reached its conclusion without properly examining the documents relating to the entire holding, and that the appellate court had settled the plaintiffs' entitlement without describing the measurement of the land at all. It held that the registered deed of gift executed at the end of 1986, produced as an exhibit, was a valid document, and that the entitlement of the OPPOSITE PARTIES had to be worked out from the documents by which the land had moved between the parties, including the earlier exchange and partition deeds. It accordingly confined the claim to twenty-eight decimals under that deed instead of the larger area found on appeal, and recalled the interim stay.",e2c78527284beb8ff9d7d0f08880cb35664a5912e389abb39555129ebf23a7f0
