The above concurrent findings by the learned courts 
below, I find that the learned courts below had to pass the 
judgments and orders in a technical manner as the plaintiff-
petitioners were indifferent about the proceeding of the suit filed 
by themselves. Both the courts below passed the respective 
judgments by way of dismissal for default. The learned appellate 
court below by his Order No. 106 dated 02.02.2009 has to pass 
the impugned judgment because of the default on the part of the 

<<PAGE 11>>
 
 
 
 
11 
Mossaddek/BO 
plaintiffs, as such, I do not find any illegality of the judgments 
and decree passed by the learned courts below where the 
plaintiffs under an obligation to get relief for a decree under the 
provisions of the Evidence Act but in the instant plaintiffs filed 
the title suit and the appeal against the impugned judgments and 
decree without committing any error of law, as such, I am not 
inclined to interfere upon the impugned judgment passed by the 
learned appellate court below. 
Accordingly, I do not find any merit in the Rule. 
In the result, the Rule is hereby discharged. 
The concerned section of this court is hereby directed to 
communicate this judgment and order to the learned courts 
below immediately. 
There is no order as to costs. 
","The PETITIONERS, plaintiffs in a suit for a decree of specific performance of a written agreement for sale of land, sought to revive the appeal by which they had challenged the decree in the suit after that appeal was lost for default. Their application under Order XLI rule 19 of the Code of Civil Procedure, with section 151, was registered as a miscellaneous case, and in that case an application for adjournment made on the footing that their witnesses were ill was not entertained, so the case proceeded no further. The PETITIONER first named had died and his heirs were not brought on record despite repeated adjournments, and no one appeared in support of the Rule. The OPPOSITE PARTY attributed the delay to tactics adopted by the plaintiffs.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,REFUSED,"The court observed that the petitions and appeals had been conducted in a technical manner because the plaintiffs were indifferent to proceedings instituted by themselves, and that the courts below had reached concurrent conclusions against them on the material placed before them. It found that the order under challenge had been passed because of the default of the plaintiffs themselves, whose advocate had failed to take the steps required, and that no illegality appeared in the manner in which the miscellaneous case had been closed. Since the record disclosed no question of law requiring interference, and the petitioners' own conduct had produced the situation, the Rule was not entertained.",62db4a62b4ebcbeb2bff71adcf47262ea82f6056457ed39ad90655af8b08c0ba
