In an application under section 115(1) of the Code of Civil Procedure, 
1908 rule was issued calling upon the opposite party Nos. 1-2 to show cause 
as to why impugned judgment and order dated 04.10.2023 passed by the 
learned Additional District Judge, 2nd Court, Sirajganj in Miscellaneous 
Appeal No. 44 of 2022 allowing the appeal and reversing the order dated 
16.08.2022 passed by the Assistant Judge, Belkuchi, Sirajganj in Other Type 
Case No. 422 of 2021 rejecting the application under Order 39 Rule 1 and 2 
of the Code of Civil Procedure, should not be set aside and/or pass such 
other or further order or orders as to this court may seem fit and proper. 
 
I have heard the learned Advocates for the petitioner as well as 
opposite parties. I have perused the impugned judgment and order passed by 
the court below, revisional application, ground taken thereon, as well as 
necessary papers and documents annexed herewith. 
On perusal of the same, it transpires that the opposite party as plaintiff 
instituted Other Type Case No. 313 of 2019 in the court of Joint District 
   Present  
          Mr. Justice Mamnoon Rahman 
 
 
 
 

<<PAGE 2>>
Judge, Sirajganj for permanent injunction impleading the petitioner as 
defendant. Subsequently, the case was transferred to the court of Assistant 
Judge, Belkuchi, Sirajganj being Title Suit No. 422 of 2021. It further 
transpires that the defendant-petitioner contested the suit by filing written 
statement denying all the material allegations made in the plaint. During 
pendency of the suit the plaintiff pressed an application under Order 39 Rule 
1 and 2 of the Code of Civil Procedure, 1908 for injunction which was 
resisted by the defendant by way of filing written objection. The trial court 
initially passed an interim order, but ultimately vide judgment and order 
dated 16.08.2022 rejected the application for injunction. Thereafter, the 
plaintiff preferred Miscellaneous Appeal being No. 44 of 2022 before the 
learned District Judge, Sirajganj and the lower appellate court after hearing 
the parties and considering the facts and circumstances allowed the 
miscellaneous case. Being aggrieved by and dissatisfied with the aforesaid 
judgment and order the petitioner moved before this court and obtained the 
present rule.  
It further transpires that at the time of issuance of rule this court 
passed interim order of status-quo in respect of possession and position of 
the suit property for a period of six months on 04.12.2023. So, it transpires 
that the interim order passed by this court is continuing for more than one 
year. Hence, I am of the view that justice would be done if a direction be 
given upon the court below to hear and dispose of the suit expeditiously, as 
possible. 
Accordingly, the court below is directed to hear and dispose of the 
suit strictly on merit by applying its independent and judicial mind 

<<PAGE 3>>
expeditiously, as possible, not later than 6(six) months from the date of 
receipt of the instant judgment and order without fail and without giving any 
adjournment to the parties. The order of status-quo shall continue till 
disposal of the suit by the court below.  
The court below is further directed to inform this court about the 
disposal of the suit as directed, in writing, through the Registrar of the High 
Court Division within 7(seven) days from the date of disposal of the suit. 
With this observation and direction, the instant rule is disposed of. 
Communicate the order at once. 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
                (Mamnoon Rahman,J:) 
","The PETITIONERS, defendants in a suit for permanent injunction over the suit property, assailed the judgment and order of the Additional District Judge, Second Court, Sirajganj in a miscellaneous appeal, being the order passed on the OPPOSITE PARTIES' application under Order XXXIX rules 1 and 2 of the Code of Civil Procedure. The OPPOSITE PARTIES, as plaintiffs, pleaded their title and peaceful possession and sought to restrain the defendants from interfering with it, and the trial court had earlier passed an interim order which it later declined to continue. The PETITIONERS contested the application by written objection and denied the plaintiffs' case. In revision they sought to undo the appellate order, and this Court noticed that its own direction to maintain status quo had been operating for over a year.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,REFUSED,"The court observed that the interim direction to maintain status quo in respect of possession and position of the suit property, issued at the time of the Rule, had continued for more than a year, and took the view that justice would be served by having the suit itself heard and decided expeditiously instead of keeping an interlocutory arrangement alive indefinitely. It accordingly directed the trial court to hear and decide the suit strictly on merit, applying its independent judicial mind, within six months of receipt of the order, without adjournments, and to report the disposal to this Court in writing through the Registrar within seven days. The status quo direction was to continue until the suit was decided.",5b4fab7b0c905fb9c2eea136aacff6fef94193699a6b3f612a4434063f76b604
