","The PETITIONERS, plaintiffs in a title suit against a development authority and the State, sought to restrain the OPPOSITE PARTIES from disturbing their possession, their application for temporary injunction under section 151 of the Code of Civil Procedure having been kept with the records by the District Judge in a civil miscellaneous appeal, which in substance left the order of the Joint District Judge, First Court, Dhaka declining that relief in the suit without disturbance. At the time of issuing the Rule this Court directed the trial court to hear the substantive application and ordered status quo meanwhile. The supplementary affidavit filed by the PETITIONERS showed that the trial court had since disposed of the application as directed, and that they had already carried the matter to the lower appellate court, where an appeal was awaiting hearing.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,REFUSED,"The court observed that the direction given at the time of issuing the Rule had already been carried out by the trial court, and that the petitioners, being dissatisfied with the order then passed on their injunction application, had invoked the jurisdiction of the lower appellate court, where their appeal was pending. In that situation it took the view that nothing survived for decision in the Rule. It declined to extend the interim arrangement further, since the trial court had decided the application and the petitioners had an alternative remedy open, and instead directed the lower appellate court to decide their appeal strictly on merit by a fixed date, with status quo as to possession meanwhile.",254636299ba6015c697d896f0b315be18f8def0703edf06a63bd3078c2440af3
