","The PETITIONERS, plaintiffs, assailed the judgment and decree of the District Judge, Chapainawabganj in a title appeal arising out of their suit, in which they sought a declaration that a registered deed of sale of 1980 relied on by the OPPOSITE PARTY was illegal, fraudulent, collusive and not binding on them. They pleaded that the deed included a non-existent parcel said to lie within the local limits of one sub-registrar's office only in order to procure registration there, no part of the real property lying within those limits, and that they had earlier purchased plots of the same holding from the original owner, mutated their names and paid rent. The OPPOSITE PARTY pleaded purchase, possession and mutation in his favour.","Other: Registration Act, 1908",ALLOWED,"The court found that the trial court had itself recorded that the plots mentioned in the disputed deed did not exist in the relevant record, yet had omitted to decide the issue whether the deed was collusive and unlawful, deciding the suit instead on the footing that the deed was earlier in time. Relying on a reported decision of the Appellate Division, it held that where property is fictitiously entered in a deed so that it may be registered where no part of the property actually exists, the registration is invalid as a fraud upon the registration law, and that the bar on a party questioning registration on that ground does not operate against a stranger.",5640fd3e0d52f3a82a619c8bd7da13058c1c88b5e035f03ff589062f48204c9e
