","The PETITIONERS, defendants in a title suit, faced a claim by the OPPOSITE PARTIES that they had acquired title to the disputed holding by remaining in hostile possession for more than twelve years, the plaintiffs pleading an unregistered agreement for sale written on stamp and cartridge paper under which they had been put in possession and had built a house and cultivated the land, and that the defendants had afterwards declined to register a deed of sale. The PETITIONERS pleaded that the plaintiffs had at best been permitted to cultivate the land for a term and had returned it, that the paper had been written up from blank signed sheets, and that the plaintiffs held no title. The trial court and the appellate court reached different conclusions.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,ALLOWED,"The court examined the schedule to the plaint and found that it gave no boundaries or other particulars sufficient to identify the land claimed, and that none of the plaintiffs' witnesses spoke to any specific demarcation, so no decree could be passed upon an unspecified parcel. It held that Order VII rule 3 of the Code of Civil Procedure requires a plaint concerning immovable property to describe it sufficiently for identification and, where boundaries or plot numbers exist in a record, to specify them, and followed decisions holding that a declaratory suit over vague and unspecified land is not maintainable. It also observed that a written agreement for sale conveys no title.",13aedd13a8716ebbb5a4693dfbf3c257d3b39552ff4860f3741715cbd16f1124
