","The PETITIONERS, plaintiffs, sued the executive authorities for a declaration that a memorandum requiring them to hand over vacant possession of a premises within seven days was illegal, collusive and not binding on them, and for a permanent injunction, pleading an unbroken chain of title through recorded settlements, a gift and two registered purchases, on the strength of which they had set up a glass business, mutated their names and paid rent and taxes, and had asked in vain for the memorandum to be withdrawn. The OPPOSITE PARTIES pleaded that the property had been acquired by the government long ago for a warehouse and that the record in the plaintiffs' favour was false. The trial court and the appellate court dealt with the suit differently.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,ALLOWED,"The court found that the plaintiffs had produced their records of rights and deeds to prove an unbroken chain of title and had also shown long exclusive possession, and that the defendants had produced no document to support their plea of acquisition even though they asserted that the records had been wrongly prepared, nor taken any step to correct them. It observed that a defendant cannot take the law into his own hands and must establish his right through due process of law, and that the trial court had dealt with the evidence in detail while the appellate court sent the case back for a fresh judgment without any cogent reason.",070fd2523b697c1566eb165d90deb230cce52a2c95255fc67d13e257e44ecd11
