","The PETITIONER, plaintiff in a partition suit over a holding recorded in an old survey, sought a separate share of a large area on the strength of two registered deeds of gift said to have been executed in his favour by the common ancestor in the nineteen forties, pleading possession and a refusal by the other side to divide the land amicably. The OPPOSITE PARTIES, the principal defendants, denied the legality and effectiveness of the deeds, pleaded that they had never been acted upon, and asserted that the plaintiff had transferred nearly all his interest to them by successive registered conveyances and retained only a small parcel. The trial court allotted shares to the plaintiff and some defendants, and the appellate court recalculated them.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,REFUSED,"The court found it admitted that the holding belonged to the common ancestor and that the plaintiff and the principal defendants were his successive heirs, and that the plaintiff had transferred his land to the defendants by several registered conveyances. It found that the appellate court had, upon a detailed analysis of the oral and documentary evidence, recalculated the shares of the plaintiff and of the defendants who had sought shares, reducing the plaintiff's allotment to the small area that remained after those transfers, and had granted separate shares to each contesting defendant. It noted that the plaintiff's own advocate conceded that no infirmity could be shown in that course, and it recalled the stay earlier granted.",0d74d577ae642ee44941ae6ccab3153e005baed68f673d54c18e1e2fdca32926
