","The PETITIONER, a husband and the defendant in a family suit, assailed the judgment and order of the Senior District Judge in a family appeal, by which the appellate court declined to disturb the decision of the Family Court allowing in part the OPPOSITE PARTY's claim for dower and maintenance. The OPPOSITE PARTY, the wife, pleaded a registered marriage deed fixing dower at a stated sum of which only a token amount was paid, that the marriage had ended by divorce, and that nothing further had been paid towards dower or her maintenance. The PETITIONER pleaded that she was quarrelsome and had of her own accord gone to live in her father's house. He came to this Court asking that the decretal money be payable in instalments by reason of his financial condition.",Other: Muslim family law,PARTLY,"The court found it admitted that the marriage had been solemnised and registered and that the dower was fixed at a stated sum of which only a nominal part had been paid, and that both courts below had rightly decreed the unpaid balance on the evidence. It observed that the maintenance fixed for the wife was modest and had been accepted having regard to the husband's means. Although it accepted that a wife is entitled to her entire deferred dower on demand and that there is ordinarily no scope for instalments, it took the petitioner's financial condition into account and permitted payment of the balance in five equal monthly instalments, with liberty to recover the whole on a single default.",5fb1c6a4887132c526bb3675d71c3e2f1b9f47c6b1ea052239b704018af68e53
