","The PETITIONERS, defendants in a suit over a holding, assailed the concurrent decisions of the courts below in favour of the OPPOSITE PARTY, who as plaintiff sought declaration of title and the setting aside of a registered deed of exchange of the mid-nineteen nineties, by which a portion of his land was said to have passed in exchange for a distant parcel, and of two later conveyances by the first defendant during the pendency of the suit. The plaintiff pleaded that the exchange deed was never read over to him, that it included more of his land than was agreed, that the distant parcel was diluviated land in which the other side had no saleable right, and that the deed was never acted upon. The PETITIONERS relied on possession, mutation and payment of rent.",Specific Relief Act 1877,REFUSED,"The court found that the power of attorney, though not dated on its face, had been notarised a little later and expressly authorised the agent to conduct legal proceedings, which Order III rule 2 of the Code of Civil Procedure permits, and that its validity had not been put to the agent in the witness box, the petitioner having used a similar power himself. It held that the dower fixed in the marriage deed stood proved, that the petitioner had produced no evidence of payment, and that a consummated marriage entitles the wife to the whole of the unpaid dower. It found the maintenance fixed for the wife and daughter, and the annual increase, appropriate.",7d1240c568f5d1e458ffc43721106e52bfd8a698bc9821a7651e238b66ab3021
