","The PETITIONER, a husband and the defendant in a family suit, assailed the judgment and decree of the Additional District Judge in a family appeal, by which the decision of the Family Court allowing the OPPOSITE PARTY's claim in part was affirmed with a modification as to the daughter's maintenance. The OPPOSITE PARTY, the wife, pleaded the marriage of 2005, dower fixed at a stated sum, the birth of a daughter, demands for money and ill treatment, the husband's departure abroad with funds taken from her, and a divorce pronounced some years later without payment of dower or maintenance. The PETITIONER pleaded that he had paid the dower and that his wife was indifferent to the child, and questioned the authority of her mother to conduct the suit under a power of attorney.",Other: Muslim family law,REFUSED,"The court found that the power of attorney, though not dated on its face, had been notarised a little later and expressly authorised the agent to conduct legal proceedings, which Order III rule 2 of the Code of Civil Procedure permits, and that its validity had not even been put to the agent in the witness box, the petitioner having used a similar power of attorney himself. It held that the dower fixed in the marriage deed stood proved, that the petitioner had produced no evidence of payment, and that a consummated marriage entitles the wife to the whole of the unpaid dower. It found the maintenance fixed for the wife and daughter, and the annual increase, appropriate.",8949bc1f9ba556d80a76dd276b6d34aafd0ab68db6aaebe5722dd080782234ec
