Anamul/BO 
 
","The PETITIONERS, whose land had been acquired for a residential project, sought a direction upon the authorities to make a specific allotment of a three-katha plot each in their favour and to hand over possession, pleading that they had applied in response to the published notice, deposited the earnest money and complied with all requirements, and that their applications had been reported missing, while the authorities kept silent for years despite decisions at the ministry and board levels and in earlier litigation involving other affected persons. The OPPOSITE PARTIES, the ministry and the development authority, resisted the claim, and a purchaser of a plot opposed any interference. Interim directions had earlier been made to preserve plots.","Other: Town Improvement Act, 1953",ALLOWED,"The court held that section 42 of the Town Improvement Act, 1953 casts a duty on the development authority to frame re-housing schemes for persons displaced by an improvement scheme, and that the obligation is mandatory, since shelter is a basic necessity and Article 15(a) of the Constitution makes its provision a fundamental responsibility of the State. It found that the allotment guidelines framed under that Act set no threshold of acquired area and place affected persons first in priority, and that earlier decisions of this Court had required rehabilitation of affected persons before any allotment to a stranger. It directed allotment of a three-katha plot to each petitioner within sixty days.",4b1f5af2d981439c719dab9afd24003fafaec951d68db1e228c1ed1f028a1695
