Heard & Judgment on: The 27th  January, 2025 
 
In an application under section 115(1) of the Code of Civil Procedure, 
1908 rule was issued calling upon the opposite parties to show cause as to 
why the impugned judgment and order dated 05.07.2023 passed by the 
learned District Judge, Rangamati in Civil Appeal No. 01 of 2020 allowing 
the appeal and thereby reversing the judgment and order dated 06.10.2019 
passed by the learned Joint District Judge, Rangamati in Civil Suit No. 649 
of 2017 allowing the application for temporary injunction to restrain the 
opposite parties from doing reformatory works over the suit land, should not 
be set aside and/or pass such other or further order or orders as to this court 
may seem fit and proper. 
 
I have heard the learned Advocates for the petitioners as well as 
opposite party Nos. 1-4. I have perused the impugned judgment and order 
passed by the court below, perused the revisional application, ground taken 
thereon as well as necessary papers and documents annexed herewith. 
On perusal of the same, it transpires that during pendency of the suit 
the trial court passed an order of injunction on the application of the 
   Present  
          Mr. Justice Mamnoon Rahman 
 
 
 
 

<<PAGE 2>>
petitioners-plaintiffs. However, on appeal the same was set aside by the 
lower appellate court on basis of the application made by the opposite party-
defendants. Against which the petitioners-plaintiffs moved before this court 
by way of revisional application under section 115(1) of the Code of Civil 
Procedure, 1908 and obtained the present rule as well as interim order. 
However, as both the parties agreed as well as I am of the view that 
justice would be done if a direction be given upon the court below to hear 
and dispose of the suit expeditiously, as possible within a fixed period. 
Accordingly, the court below is directed to hear and dispose of the suit 
strictly on merit by applying its independent and judicial mind within 30th 
June, 2025 without fail and without giving any adjournment to the parties. In 
the meantime, the parties are directed to maintain status-quo in respect of 
possession and position of the suit property till disposal of the suit by the 
court below. 
With this observation and direction, the instant rule is disposed of. 
Communicate the order at once. 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
                (Mamnoon Rahman,J:) 
","The PETITIONERS, plaintiffs in a suit over a disputed holding, assailed the judgment and order of the District Judge in a civil appeal by which the interim order of injunction passed in their favour during the pendency of the suit, restraining the OPPOSITE PARTIES from carrying out works on the land, was displaced at the instance of the defendants. The OPPOSITE PARTIES maintained that the order could not stand. Both sides ultimately agreed that the real remedy lay in an early decision of the suit itself, and this Court considered whether the interlocutory arrangement should be kept alive or the suit directed to be heard and decided within a fixed period.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,PARTLY,"UNCERTAIN: the Rule was disposed of with a direction and the appellate order was not disturbed, so the classification is doubtful. The court observed that the dispute arose during the pendency of the suit from an order of injunction granted on the plaintiffs' application and later displaced on the defendants' application, and that the parties were agreed that an early disposal would serve the ends of justice. It accordingly directed the trial court to hear and decide the suit strictly on merit, applying independent and judicial mind, by a fixed date and without adjournments, the parties maintaining the position and possession of the property until the suit was decided.",46e4cb13e27ea643cdeecfba6f8835d456e676408651b45af3b01f221460a36d
