","The PETITIONERS, purchasers, assailed the judgment and order of the Additional District Judge in a miscellaneous appeal by which the pre-emption case decided in favour of the OPPOSITE PARTIES was left undisturbed. The OPPOSITE PARTIES had pleaded that they were co-sharers by inheritance in the holding, that one of the vendors had sold a parcel to the PETITIONERS by a registered conveyance without informing them or serving notice, that the purchasers were strangers, and that they had learnt of the transaction from a neighbour and had obtained a certified copy before filing the case within the prescribed period. The PETITIONERS pleaded that the land had been offered to the pre-emptors, who declined it, and that the case was barred by limitation and for defect of parties.","Other: State Acquisition and Tenancy Act, 1950",REFUSED,"The court found that the pre-emptors were co-sharers in the holding, that the purchasers were strangers to it, and that the case had been instituted within the period prescribed by law, since they had learnt of the transfer from a witness who deposed in support of their case and had obtained a certified copy of the conveyance shortly before filing. It found no reliable evidence for the purchasers' case that the land had been offered to the pre-emptors, who declined it, and observed that the witness produced on their behalf to that effect was not corroborated. It found no error of law in the concurrent findings.",8739cafd15f92baa634d404bbc6c4a33022b0e26d5cc26d98aa084eaa8c3baff
