","The PETITIONERS, defendants in a partition suit, assailed the concurrent decisions of the courts below allotting a separate share to the OPPOSITE PARTIES on the strength of two registered conveyances of the early nineteen nineties from the common predecessor, who had herself purchased the land in the seventies and whose holding had been recorded in the older survey. The PETITIONERS pleaded that the land actually available in the plot was less than what had been recorded, that the plaintiffs had taken their land from other plots and were claiming the whole from this one, and that the suit as framed took only a part of the recorded holding into account while leaving out the rest. The OPPOSITE PARTIES supported the allotment made in their favour.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,PARTLY,"The court found it admitted that the plaintiffs had purchased a specified area from the common predecessor, but that the plaint had brought into the common stock only part of the land recorded in the holding instead of the whole of it, and that a suit for partition must include the entire holding and all the co-sharers, failing which it cannot proceed. It held that the plaintiffs' case that the area of the plot had been reduced in the village map was misconceived, since title and quantity are established by the title deed and the record of rights. It remanded the suit for retrial after amendment.",99df6ebe1b4019935c9cf2e81d8703b099293feae52271d8f34c0c63bc4145e8
