","The opposite parties as plaintiffs sued for declaration of title to about 2.19 acres, asserting that the land originally belonged to a recorded tenant whose son and daughter dedicated a share of it by a registered deed of waqf, and that the suit land had been wrongly recorded in the names of two other persons from whom the defendants claimed descent and purchase. The defendants contested, asserting that the settlement records in their predecessors' names were correct and that they possessed the land by paying rent. The trial court decreed the suit and the appellate court concurred in the appeal, whereupon the defendants moved this Division in revision, urging deficiency of pleadings and want of proof of the plaintiffs' title and possession.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,ALLOWED,"The court found that the waqf deed, the sole source of the plaintiffs' title, covered about 6.34 acres, whereas the plaintiffs already held 4.35 acres under it and by claiming a further 2.19 acres were asserting a total exceeding the deed. Increased area shown in later survey could not extend the plaintiffs' title. The plaint did not disclose the total land of the original owner or even the location and boundaries of the disputed plots. Both sides having jointly submitted that the pleadings and evidence were deficient, the court upset the concurrent judgments and remanded the suit for retrial, permitting amendment of pleadings and fresh evidence, with status quo and a six-month limit.",e924a725492ae26372b4aca36201e3f14770ad5ecd908a553709157782e4755d
