","The plaintiffs instituted a suit seeking a declaration that an ex parte decree passed in an earlier title suit in 1970 was not binding upon them and had been obtained by fraud, asserting that they had not been made parties and had learnt of the decree only upon the defendants attempting to dispossess them. The defendants denied the allegations, claiming title through a pattan from the former landlords and asserting that they had lawfully obtained the earlier decree against the government and had long possessed the land. The trial court decided the suit against the plaintiffs while the appellate court reversed that decision, and the defendants-petitioners moved this Division in revision against the reversal.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,ALLOWED,"The court found the plaintiffs' case riddled with contradictions as to the survey khatian number, the plot number and the year of settlement recorded in the plaint, the deposition and the rent receipts. The certified settlement documents showed the plaintiffs' predecessor had settled a different plot and acreage, the plot claimed being the defendants' holding covered by the 1970 decree. No evidence proved fraud or collusion in obtaining that decree, though section 90 of the Evidence Act and the cited apex court decisions placed that burden on the plaintiffs. A suit for declaration simpliciter, without consequential relief, was also not maintainable. The appellate judgment, being no proper judgment of reversal, was upset and the trial court's decree restored.",84aef7c488272eecf5951560698519d0e4a21f785a4f4c44c39a38cadd509d88
