","The plaintiffs sued for declaration of title to, and redemption of, about 1.03 acres, pleading that their predecessors had borrowed money on registered deeds of conditional sale with an agreement for reconveyance within five years, that reconveyance had been promised but later declined, and that they had been threatened with dispossession. The defendant sought striking out of the plaint, contending that the plaintiffs had earlier moved a land-office proceeding for release of the property, that the order made in their favour had been declared of no legal effect by this Division in a writ petition, and that a fresh suit on the same cause was barred. The plaint was struck out and the appellate court concurred; the plaintiffs moved this Division in revision.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,REFUSED,"The court held that the earlier release order obtained by the plaintiffs had been annulled in a writ petition, where this Division held the mortgage transactions closed and the property no longer redeemable, and that the plaintiffs had not carried the matter further. The subject matter, the parties and the issue in the present suit being substantially the same, the suit was barred by res judicata under section 11 of the Code of Civil Procedure, 1908. Relying on apex court decisions including the power under section 151, the court held that a plaint may be struck out where the suit is hopeless, and found the suit barred under Order VII rule 11(d) as well. The rule accordingly failed.",e518f942e5de4123bc2cb706528aa6694a61c16716afb10a8df264dca596928d
