","The opposite party wife had instituted a family suit against the petitioner husband before the Family Court, and the matter was carried on appeal before the District Judge. Still dissatisfied, the petitioner moved this Division in revision, assailing the judgment and decree passed in the family appeal. When the case came up for pronouncement, both the petitioner and the opposite party appeared before this court in person and stated that they were willing to settle their dispute amicably outside the court. A supplementary affidavit annexing a written memorandum of settlement was filed on behalf of the opposite party and duly endorsed by the learned advocate for the petitioner.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,PARTLY,"The court heard the learned advocates of both sides as well as the parties themselves, and examined the conditions set out in the memorandum of settlement appended to the supplementary affidavit. On perusal, the court found the terms to be lawful and valid and both parties were agreed to them. Neither the decree under challenge nor the relief originally sought was adjudicated on merits; the controversy was instead brought to an end by consent, the revisional rule being disposed of without any order as to costs and the settlement annexed to the affidavit being treated as part of the decree. The stay earlier ordered was recalled.",f4df88bd7fe607216bb1cbca871adb4a427fad98cbd79daae5922717e4135477
