and Killburn, 33DLR (AD) 298 
","The petitioner concern opened a letter of credit for importing optical fibre cables from a foreign supplier and alleged that the goods delivered were of inferior quality, contrary to the terms of the contract. It thereupon instituted a suit against the supplier for a declaration, permanent injunction and compensation, and applied for temporary injunction. The trial court declined the application, holding that a letter of credit is independent of the underlying contract of sale and that stopping payment would adversely affect international trade. The appellate court took the same view, and the plaintiff-petitioner carried the matter to this Division in revision.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,REFUSED,"Reviewing the authorities here and abroad on the autonomy of letters of credit under Article 4 of UCP 600, the court held that banks deal in documents alone and must honour a credit where the documents conform. The buyer had raised no discrepancy with the bank before taking delivery of the goods, and a bare allegation of fraud in the quality of the goods, unsupported by clear evidence, was not enough to restrain payment. Since the suit itself claimed compensation, pecuniary damages could adequately cover any loss, and the balance of convenience did not favour an injunction. The courts below were found to have acted rightly, and the rule failed.",6b7a83d3ff4fb5424c4d5c2700ccc6c7f2b18458beb5c573af541bc366f1b1bb
