","The petitioner, a ship-breaking concern, had obtained a rule from this Division in a civil revision arising out of a money suit that was then pending, seeking to keep a bank guarantee from being encashed while the suit remained undisposed of. An interim order had been made at the time of issuance of the rule and had been extended from time to time until disposal of the rule. When the matter came up for hearing, none appeared for either side. The record disclosed that the suit had been instituted in 1991 and that there was no information on record regarding its fate.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,PARTLY,"The court noticed that the suit was of the year 1991 and that no update concerning it was available, and considered that in such circumstances the appropriate course was a direction to the court below rather than an adjudication of the revisional rule on its merits. The trial court was accordingly directed to hear and dispose of the suit strictly on merit within six months of receipt of the order, if it had not already been disposed of. The interim order earlier passed in respect of encashment of the bank guarantee was directed to continue until disposal of the suit. The rule was disposed of with those observations.",7b6f2d0c7df86f1e31a979aff023ae876b945feba120ee8fde497a0ddc424f8f
