","The plaintiff sued for cancellation of a registered deed by which the disputed land stood transferred to the defendant, alleging that she had gone to the registration office believing she was executing a power of attorney, that the contents were never read over to her, and that she learnt of the gift only later, upon the defendant disclosing it and a certified copy being obtained. The defendant denied the allegations, asserting that the deed had been executed voluntarily with full knowledge, in the presence of witnesses, and that the plaintiff had herself delivered possession. The trial court decided the suit against the plaintiff, while the appellate court took the opposite view.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,ALLOWED,"The court found that the execution of the registered deed and the plaintiff's signature and thumb impression were admitted, that no corroborative witness was produced to establish the alleged fraud, and that a registered instrument carries a strong presumption of genuineness. The findings recorded by the appellate court on want of independent advice and on failure to deliver possession were outside the averments in the plaint, and the observation about delivery of possession in a gift was contrary to the rule that a registered deed of gift suffices under the governing personal law. The reversal not having displaced the trial court's findings, the trial court's decree was restored.",0c205141dc63d0f646f5c13f6fef2e61a94418c9f38cf3fbca3fa957999940f3
