","The plaintiffs instituted a suit for declaration of title in respect of one schedule of land and for partition of another, pleading that their predecessor had acquired an undivided share in a large tract through a registered kabala deed executed by an attorney of the former owners, that only a small part of her share had been recorded in her name in the settlement records, and that the defendants, though co-sharers, had no right to the balance. The defendants resisted the claim, asserting that the shares had been acquired in proportion to the lands each recipient had given in exchange and that the record was correct. Both courts below took differing views.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,ALLOWED,"The court found that the schedules of the plaint suffered from serious deficiencies: the total tract of about 27.05 acres had not been brought into the hotchpotch, and the schedule relating to the declaratory relief had not been specified by plots or boundaries, so that no effective decree could follow even if title to the claimed share were established. The learned advocate for the opposite parties conceded the defects, attributing them to the drafting of the plaint. Both sides having sought a fresh trial, the appellate decree was upset and the suit remanded for retrial, with liberty to amend the pleadings and adduce evidence, within six months.",338fba4d6df1e762b741cfc4d63dfc85238440a2f0fb04f5bb099bff294b5d15
