Md. Aminul Islam, J: 
 
I agree. 
","The mother and two daughters of a family instituted a suit for compensation against a man, alleging that he had denied his parenthood of the youngest plaintiff, thereby casting aspersions on the mother's chastity and humiliating all three of them socially, and claiming a very large sum divided among them. During the pendency of the suit the man applied for a DNA test of himself and the child, and a government forensic laboratory conducted the test and submitted a report. He then filed a written objection to that report and prayed that a further DNA test be carried out at a laboratory abroad.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,REFUSED,"The court recorded that during the hearing the man's advocate candidly admitted before it that the third plaintiff is his daughter, and that at an alternative dispute resolution session held at the court's initiative he again admitted the child's parentage, apologised to all three plaintiffs and acknowledged his duties and liabilities towards the two daughters, though he declined to make amends to the mother. In that situation the court held that the facts and law bearing on paternity no longer required discussion, and that no further test was called for. The trial court was directed to dispose of the suit confined to the claim for compensation within six months.",71e16b5226e9bf614078d8c810188a48dfda94262fd8f2e5a8b7de4f106f93fc
