","The petitioner, a grandmother, was the defendant in a family suit brought by her daughter-in-law, who sought to be appointed the legal guardian of her minor son so that she could deal with the minor's property. The trial court appointed the mother guardian and, on appeal by the grandmother, that decision was confirmed. The grandmother then carried the matter to this Division by way of revision, opposing the mother's subsequent application for permission to sell the minor's land, which was said to be needed for the child's maintenance. While the revisional rule was awaiting pronouncement, the parties filed a joint application stating that they had settled the matter.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,PARTLY,"The court perused the compromise arrived at between the parties, by which they had partitioned their joint property under the personal law applicable to them and agreed on distribution of the rental income. It was observed that the mother, the father of the minor having died, is his natural guardian, so her appointment was proper, though such appointment by itself would not entitle her to dispose of the minor's property. Since the compromise, once acted upon, would make any sale permission unnecessary, the decree was modified and the agreement made part of it, parties being directed to act within two weeks and to register it within three months.",fdeb33eedb59f8f81e37c6abf7b8197168cadb37cf6f5f7eca69ab452e1fb14c
