Obaidul Hasan/B.O. 
","The plaintiffs sued for declaration of title, recovery of possession and a further declaration that two auction sales of the nineteen-thirties and forties, together with a sale certificate and a rent sale document relied on by the defendants, were collusive and not binding on them. Their case was that the land descended to them by inheritance from the recorded tenants, that a wrong record had been prepared in the defendants' names, and that they had been forcibly dispossessed. The defendants claimed through their predecessor, who was said to have purchased the property at a certificate sale held for arrears of government revenue.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,REFUSED,"The court found the sale certificate relied on by the defendants defective: it was issued in the form prescribed for a civil court sale though the alleged proceeding was a certificate case for revenue arrears, which would require a different form and procedure, and it bore a survey khatian number although that record had been finally published after the alleged sale. The defendants never called for the original records nor produced other reliable material, so the burden under the Evidence Act was not met, while the plaintiffs' title and possession of the disputed area and their dispossession were established. The appellate decision was left undisturbed and the rule failed.",8f0b34e7519a048054d25ca4fc90360c7f02cf77635268ac01b4626d122f12db
