","The petitioner instituted a pre-emption case claiming a preferential right over about 24.5 decimals of non-agricultural land held in the same holding, asserting that the first opposite party was a stranger and that the land had in truth been sold to him for money, the deed having been dressed up as one of exchange to defeat the petitioner's right, and that no notice of the transfer had been served. The opposite parties maintained that the transaction was a genuine exchange, each side having received land in return, and that the deed had been duly acted upon by mutation.","Other: Non-Agricultural Tenancy Act, 1949",REFUSED,"The court noted that the statutory provision governing pre-emption expressly leaves a deed of exchange outside its scope, and that the instrument in question had been drawn, executed and registered as an exchange, the consideration for land being other land and not money. The petitioner had nowhere stated the amount of consideration in the pleadings, had accepted the valuation recited in the deed, and one of his two attesting witnesses admitted in cross-examination that he was neither present at the negotiations nor at the reading over of the deed, while the other's version of a money payment was beyond the pleadings and uncorroborated. The rule failed.",dc0b0a1ae9955db3b6516b8a46460e37216c88d731150ccc87f3c31f5dab2591
